Q: Disable Laravel&#39;s Eloquent timestamps A: <p>To disable Eloquent timestamps for a model, set the <code>$timestamps</code> property to <code>false</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

class Post extends Model
{
    public $timestamps = false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>That's it.</p>
<p>When <code>$timestamps = false</code>, Eloquent will not try to automatically maintain the <code>created_at</code> and <code>updated_at</code> columns for that model, so those columns are not required in the table.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Laravel 13+ alternative</h2>
<p>In Laravel 13+, you can also use the <code>WithoutTimestamps</code> attribute:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Attributes\WithoutTimestamps;
use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

#[WithoutTimestamps]
class Post extends Model
{
    //
}
</code></pre>
<p>This requires a Laravel version that supports <code>WithoutTimestamps</code> and a PHP version that supports attributes.</p>
<p>For older Laravel versions, use:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>public $timestamps = false;
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h2>Disable timestamps for all models</h2>
<p>If you want this behaviour across all your models, create a base model:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

namespace App\Models;

use Illuminate\Database\Eloquent\Model;

abstract class BaseModel extends Model
{
    public $timestamps = false;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then extend your models from it:</p>
<pre class="lang-php prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;?php

namespace App\Models;

class Post extends BaseModel
{
    //
}
</code></pre>

Q: art::ConditionVariable::WaitHoldingLocks(art::Thread*) A: <p>I ran into the same issue a while ago. Firebase crash reports pointing to <code>libart.so</code> are frustrating because the stack trace usually does not reference your actual Java/Kotlin code directly.</p>
<p>The key line in your stack trace is likely something similar to:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>art::ThreadList::WaitForOtherNonDaemonThreadsToExit
</code></pre>
<p>What this usually means is:</p>
<p>When the app is shutting down, Android tries to terminate the JVM cleanly. However, one or more non-daemon background threads are still running, so the main thread waits for them to finish. If those threads never terminate, the app hangs during shutdown and eventually triggers an ANR.</p>
<p>In many cases, this happens because of:</p>
<ul>
<li>Long-running custom threads</li>
<li>Infinite loops (<code>while(true)</code>)</li>
<li>WakeLocks / <code>FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON</code></li>
<li>Socket listeners or polling loops</li>
<li>Improper crash handlers</li>
</ul>
<p><code>FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON</code> itself is usually not the direct cause. The real issue is often the background work associated with it that never gets cleaned up properly.</p>
<h2>Things to check</h2>
<h3>1. Make sure custom threads are terminated properly</h3>
<p>If you manually create threads, ensure they are interrupted/stopped correctly.</p>
<p>You can also mark non-critical worker threads as daemon threads so they do not block JVM shutdown.</p>
<h4>Kotlin</h4>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>val workerThread = Thread {
    // Background task
}

workerThread.isDaemon = true
workerThread.start()
</code></pre>
<h4>Java</h4>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Thread workerThread = new Thread(() -&gt; {
    // Background task
});

workerThread.setDaemon(true);
workerThread.start();
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Note: <code>setDaemon(true)</code> must be called before <code>start()</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That said, blindly converting every thread to daemon is not always the best solution. The more important part is ensuring threads exit cleanly when the Activity/Service is destroyed.</p>
<hr />
<h3>2. Clean up resources in <code>onDestroy()</code></h3>
<p>If you use WakeLocks, listeners, handlers, executors, or long-running loops, release them explicitly.</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>override fun onDestroy() {
    super.onDestroy()

    // Release WakeLock
    if (wakeLock?.isHeld == true) {
        wakeLock.release()
    }

    // Clear screen-on flag
    window.clearFlags(WindowManager.LayoutParams.FLAG_KEEP_SCREEN_ON)

    // Stop background thread
    workerThread?.interrupt()
}
</code></pre>
<p>Also verify that your loop/thread actually respects interruption:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted) {
    // work
}
</code></pre>
<hr />
<h3>3. Check custom crash handlers</h3>
<p>A very common mistake is swallowing uncaught exceptions inside a custom crash handler.</p>
<p>If you intercept crashes but never forward them to the system handler, the process may not terminate correctly.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>Thread.UncaughtExceptionHandler defaultHandler = Thread.getDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler();

Thread.setDefaultUncaughtExceptionHandler((thread, throwable) -&gt; {

    // Log crash here

    // Forward to Android default handler
    if (defaultHandler != null) {
        defaultHandler.uncaughtException(thread, throwable);
    }
});
</code></pre>
<h2>Summary</h2>
<p>Search your project for:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>new Thread</code></li>
<li><code>Executors</code></li>
<li><code>WakeLock</code></li>
<li><code>while(true)</code></li>
<li>Socket listeners / polling loops</li>
</ul>
<p>Then verify:</p>
<ul>
<li>Threads can terminate correctly</li>
<li>Resources are released in lifecycle callbacks</li>
<li>Crash handlers forward exceptions properly</li>
</ul>
<p>That resolved the issue in my case.</p>
